# TypeScript Standards

## No `as` type assertions

Never use `as SomeType` to override the compiler — it silently lies about a
value's shape and defers crashes to runtime. Fix the type at its source instead.

`as const` is fine (it narrows/freezes, it doesn't override). `as unknown as T`
double-casts are banned too.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — lying to the compiler
const config = JSON.parse(raw) as ModConfig;
const data = value as unknown as Manifest;

// ✅ GOOD — validate / narrow with zod, or type the source
const config = modConfigSchema.parse(JSON.parse(raw)); // returns ModConfig, throws on mismatch
if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && 'id' in value) { /* narrowed */ }

// ✅ GOOD — `as const` is allowed
const MODES = ['install', 'remove'] as const;
```

Prefer zod schemas, type guards (`instanceof`, `in`, `typeof`), and discriminated
unions over assertions. Reserve `as` only for genuinely unavoidable interop, and
add a comment explaining why.

## Prefer precise types over `any`

- Use `unknown` for untrusted input (parsed JSON/YAML, file contents), then
  narrow — ideally by passing it through a zod schema. Never use `any` to silence
  errors.
- Avoid non-null assertions (`value!`); guard explicitly instead.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function readEntry(payload: any) { return payload.id; }

// ✅ GOOD
function readEntry(payload: unknown) {
  return entrySchema.parse(payload).id;
}
```

## Explicit return types

Annotate an explicit return type on functions wherever possible — always on
exported/public functions, and on non-trivial local ones. It documents intent
and catches accidental changes to the return shape at the definition instead of
at some distant call site.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — return type inferred
const loadConfig = async () => {
  /* ... */
}

// ✅ GOOD — explicit
const loadConfig = async (): Promise<ModConfig> => {
  /* ... */
}
```

Exception: trivial inline callbacks where the type is obvious (e.g.
`files.map((f) => f.name)`). Prefer letting zod infer schema types via
`z.infer<typeof schema>` rather than restating them by hand.

## General conventions

- Prefer `type`/`interface` and discriminated unions over loose object shapes.
- Use `readonly` and `const` by default; avoid mutation where practical.
- No `// @ts-ignore`; use `// @ts-expect-error` with a reason if truly needed.
